EXPERIMENT 5
Create a Basic GitHub Actions Workflow
1. Aim
To create a basic GitHub Actions workflow that automatically runs a task whenever code is pushed to the GitHub repository.
2. Software Requirements
GitHub account
Web browser
Internet connection
GitHub repository
3. Procedure — Step by Step
Step 1: Open GitHub
Open your browser.
Go to GitHub⁠�.
Log in to your GitHub account.
Step 2: Open the Repository
Open your repository: GitExperiment1 Repository⁠�.
Click the Code tab.
Find the .github folder.
Open .github.
Open the workflows folder.
Step 3: Create a Workflow File
Click Add file.
Select Create new file.
In the filename box, type:
basic-ci.yml
Note: The full path will be .github/workflows/basic-ci.yml because you are creating the file inside the workflows folder.
Step 4: Write the Workflow Code
Copy and paste the following code into the file editor:
name: Basic CI Workflow

on:
  push:
    branches: ["main"]
  pull_request:
    branches: ["main"]

jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - name: Checkout repository
        uses: actions/checkout@v3

      - name: Display a message
        run: echo "Hello, GitHub Actions!"
Explanation:
name: Specifies the workflow name.
on: Defines when the workflow runs.
push: Runs the workflow when code is pushed to main.
pull_request: Runs the workflow when a pull request targets main.
jobs: Defines the tasks to perform.
build: Names the job.
runs-on: Selects an Ubuntu virtual machine.
actions/checkout@v3: Checks out the repository code.
run: Executes the command.
echo: Displays a message in the log.
Step 5: Commit the Workflow File
Click Commit changes….
Enter this commit message:
Create basic CI workflow
Select Commit directly to the main branch.
Click Commit changes.
The workflow file is now saved in your repository.
Step 6: Open GitHub Actions
Go to the repository.
Click the Actions tab.
Find Basic CI Workflow on the left side.
Click the latest workflow run.
Open the build job.
Step 7: Check the Output
Open the Display a message step.
Verify that the log displays:
Hello, GitHub Actions!
A green tick (✓) means the workflow completed successfully.
Step 8: Verify the Workflow
Confirm the following:
The basic-ci.yml file exists in .github/workflows.
The workflow appears in the Actions tab.
The build job has a green tick.
The output displays Hello, GitHub Actions!.
Expected output:
Hello, GitHub Actions!
4. Algorithm
Start.
Open GitHub and log in.
Open the repository.
Create a workflow file inside .github/workflows.
Write the YAML configuration.
Commit the file to the main branch.
GitHub Actions automatically starts the workflow.
Check the job status and output.
Stop.
5. Result
Thus, a basic GitHub Actions workflow was created and successfully executed. The workflow automatically ran on a push to the main branch and displayed the message Hello, GitHub Actions!.
